.\" Man page for supersonic. Keep the option list in step with the parser
.\" (parseArgs) and the --help text (usage) in host/EngineHost.cpp, and with
.\" the engine's own options in dsp/scsynth/scsynth_options.h. The parser is
.\" what the flags do; check a statement there before writing it here.
.TH SUPERSONIC 1 "October 2026" "supersonic" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
supersonic \- low-latency scsynth-compatible audio synthesis server
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B supersonic
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B supersonic
is a port of SuperCollider's scsynth audio engine focused on low latency and
long-session reliability. It accepts scsynth command-line flags and the
scsynth OSC command set, so SuperCollider clients (such as Sonic Pi) can
launch and drive it as a drop-in replacement for
.BR scsynth (1).
.PP
Commands arrive as OSC over UDP by default, on 127.0.0.1 only. Alternative
command transports (TCP, Unix sockets, a Windows named pipe, shared memory)
can be selected with the transport options below. Ableton Link session sync
(including Link Audio streaming), MIDI I/O, game controller input, and OSC cue
networking are built in; a client turns them on, or subscribes to them,
with OSC commands.
.PP
The log goes to standard error. There is no log file.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Audio
.TP
.BI \-H " name"
.TQ
.BI \-H " in out"
Audio device. One name names the output and the input; two name the input
and then the output. The output is a fuzzy match on
\[dq]Driver : Device\[dq], as
.B \-\-list\-devices
prints it: every word must appear, case ignored, and the shortest match
wins. The input name must be the device's name as listed.
.B __system__
as the output follows the system default output, as giving no
.B \-H
does. The named device is remembered by that name: while it is missing or
unplugged the server plays on the default, and it switches back when a device
of that name appears.
.TP
.BI \-S " rate"
Sample rate (default: 48000). If the device does not offer it, the device's
own rate is kept.
.TP
.BI \-Z " size"
Hardware buffer size (default: automatic: the smallest size of 128 or more
that the device offers).
.TP
.BI \-z " size"
DSP control block size, clamped to 32\-1024 (default: automatic: the device's
buffer size when it is 32 to 128, otherwise 128).
.TP
.BI \-i " num"
Input channels (default: all the device has; 0 turns inputs off).
.TP
.BI \-o " num"
Output channels (default: all the device has).
.TP
.BI \-\-audio\-driver " name"
Driver to boot on, such as CoreAudio, Windows Audio, DirectSound, ASIO,
PipeWire, ALSA or JACK: the exact name, or a unique case-insensitive match.
An unknown name is logged and the platform's default kept. ASIO needs a
device named with
.BR \-H .
.TP
.B \-\-list\-devices
List the audio devices, with their channels, sample rates and buffer sizes,
and exit. Other options are ignored.
.TP
.B \-\-headless
No audio device; a timer renders the audio and OSC is still answered. For
tests and CI.
.SS Network
.TP
.BI \-u " port"
UDP command port (default: 57110). It also numbers the shared-memory segment;
.B \-u 0
opens neither.
.TP
.BI \-B " addr"
Address the UDP and TCP command ports bind (default: 127.0.0.1).
.B 0.0.0.0
binds every IPv4 interface. An empty address binds every IPv4 and IPv6
interface for UDP, and every IPv4 interface for TCP.
.SS Engine
Each value goes to the engine, which refuses one outside its range with a
log line naming it. The process then stays up without a running synth.
.TP
.BI \-b " num"
Sample buffers (default: 1024; 1\-65535).
.TP
.BI \-n " num"
Nodes (synths and groups) that may exist at once (default: 1024).
.TP
.BI \-d " num"
Synth definitions that may be loaded at once (default: 1024).
.TP
.BI \-w " num"
Wire buffers for a synth's internal connections (default: 64).
.TP
.BI \-a " num"
Audio bus channels (default: 1024).
.TP
.BI \-c " num"
Control bus channels (default: 16384).
.TP
.BI \-m " size"
Real-time memory pool in KB (default: 8192). The engine's heap is sized to
hold it.
.TP
.BI \-r " num"
Random number generators (default: 64).
.TP
.BI \-D " 0|1"
Load the synth definitions in the synthdef directory at boot, before the
command port opens (default: 1). The directory is each one in
.B SC_SYNTHDEF_PATH
when that is set, else
.I synthdefs
in the user's application-support directory.
.SS Other
.TP
.BI \-\-default\-bpm " n"
Opening session tempo (default: 120).
.TP
.BI \-\-app\-name " name"
Name published to the operating system's audio and MIDI registries: PipeWire
nodes, ALSA sequencer clients, macOS aggregate devices and Link peers
(default: SuperSonic).
.TP
.BI \-\-inbox\-mb " n"
The inbox lane, where loaded samples and other assets live, in MB
(default: 512; 1\-3072). Address space, not memory: pages are committed as
they are written.
.TP
.B \-v
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the options and exit.
.TP
\fB\-U\fP, \fB\-R\fP, \fB\-l\fP, \fB\-I\fP, \fB\-O\fP \fIvalue\fP
scsynth's options, accepted and ignored.
.PP
An unknown option is reported in the log and skipped.
.SS Command transports
Pick at most one; it replaces the UDP command port. The cue server and
outbound OSC are unaffected, and
.B \-u
still numbers the shared-memory segment.
.TP
.BI \-\-tcp " port"
TCP; each OSC packet is preceded by its length as a 4-byte big-endian
integer. Binds the
.B \-B
address.
.TP
.BI \-\-uds " path"
Unix socket, stream, framed as for TCP (macOS and Linux; file mode 0600).
.TP
.BI \-\-uds\-dgram " path"
Unix socket, datagram, one OSC packet each (macOS and Linux; file mode 0600).
A client that wants replies binds a path of its own.
.TP
.BI \-\-pipe " name"
Windows named pipe
.RI \[rs]\[rs].\[rs]pipe\[rs] name ,
framed as for TCP. Only the user running the server (and SYSTEM) may
connect, and remote clients are refused.
.TP
.B \-\-shm\-commands
The shared-memory segment's peer command plane, for one trusted peer on the
same machine (requires
.B \-u
> 0).
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-connections " n"
Connection cap for TCP, Unix stream sockets and named pipes (default: 4;
1\-1024).
.SS Shared memory
With
.B \-u
> 0 the engine's memory is a shared-memory segment, served to processes of
the same user at an attach endpoint.
.TP
.BI \-\-shm\-endpoint " path|pipe"
Where to serve it. The default is
.RI clockwork\-shm\- port .sock
in
.BR $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR ,
else
.BR $TMPDIR ,
else
.RI /tmp/clockwork\-shm\- uid \- port .sock;
on Windows,
.RI \[rs]\[rs].\[rs]pipe\[rs]clockwork\-shm\- port .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B CLOCKWORK_DEV_LOG
Set to 1 for extra developer log lines (any value counts but an empty
one or one starting with 0).
.TP
.B CLOCKWORK_PLUGIN_BRIDGE
Path of the plugin bridge, in a build with plugin hosting. Otherwise it is
looked for beside the server, then in the directory the build was configured
with.
.SH SIGNALS
SIGINT and SIGTERM stop the server cleanly, as does the OSC command
.BR /quit ;
it exits with status 0. A crash
prints a FATAL line naming the signal, with a backtrace on macOS and Linux,
and exits with status 128 plus the signal number.
.SH EXIT STATUS
1 when two command transports are given;
.B \-\-shm\-commands
or
.B \-\-shm\-endpoint
is given with
.BR "\-u 0" ;
the engine cannot start; or a TCP, Unix socket, named pipe or shared-memory
command transport cannot start. A UDP port that cannot be bound is logged and
the server carries on.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR scsynth (1),
.BR jackd (1)
.PP
The native server guide, docs/NATIVE.md, and the OSC reference,
docs/OSC_API.md, in the source tree.
.PP
